Title:
Needle in the haystack: LLMs for vulnerability research

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4–8 sentences max):
- This post describes a pragmatic methodology for using LLMs (e.g., Codex/Claude) to find real vulnerabilities in large codebases without manual source review.  
- It argues that “find all vulnerabilities” prompts fail due to missing threat models and long-context degradation (“context rot” / lost-in-the-middle), producing noisy, generic findings.  
- The proposed approach is minimal persistent scaffolding (a compact threat model + invariants) combined with “thin-slice” audits focused on concrete trust boundaries and high-risk operations, then verification via tests/harnesses.  
- It includes case studies where this workflow reportedly produced dozens of issues/CVEs across popular OSS projects (Parse Server authz, Hono JWT/JWKS alg confusion, Elysia cookie signature logic, harden-runner syscall coverage gaps, BullFrog DNS/firewall bypasses, Better-Hub XSS/cache/OAuth issues).  
- The post also catalogs effective “prompt injection” patterns for vuln research (asserting a bug exists, asking for PoCs, adversarial framing, invariant-violation prompts, iterative “what else?”).  
- Useful for red teamers, pentesters, and security researchers building LLM-assisted audit pipelines; also relevant to defenders evaluating LLM audit reliability and verification needs.

Technical Focus:
- Threat modeling as context compression (trust boundaries, attacker models, invariants)
- Long-context failure modes (context rot, primacy/recency, lost-in-the-middle)
- Slice-based code auditing (entrypoints → sensitive sinks mapping)
- LLM-guided vulnerability discovery + verifier loops (tests, harnesses, repro)
- Auth/authz and crypto footguns (JWT/JWKS alg confusion, audience validation)
- Egress-control bypass analysis (syscall instrumentation gaps, DNS/firewall edge cases)

Use Cases:
- Build an LLM-assisted workflow to triage large repos by auditing one attack surface at a time (auth, parsing, deserialization, CI tooling).
- Generate and iteratively refine a project-specific threat model from prior CVEs/advisories to guide targeted audits.
- Use LLMs to enumerate invariants and systematically search for invariant violations across handlers/routes.
- Produce concrete PoCs/minimal test cases to reduce false positives and speed disclosure/patching.
- Assess and harden security tools (CI egress controls) by modeling bypass classes (syscall gaps, DNS parsing, shared-IP hosting).

Keywords:
LLM-assisted code audit, vulnerability research, threat model, trust boundaries, invariants, context rot, lost-in-the-middle, primacy recency, OpenAI Codex, Claude Opus, Firefox JS engine, CVE-2026-29182, CVE-2026-30228, CVE-2026-30229, CVE-2026-30863, JWT, JWKS, algorithm confusion, HS256, audience claim bypass, authorization bypass, cookie signature validation, syscall instrumentation, sendto/sendmsg/sendmmsg, DNS pipelining, egress filtering bypass, XSS, cache authorization bypass, OAuth token exposure